From 4.4, the fission energy release per \(^{235}\mathrm{U}\) nucleus is

\[
\Delta E = 185.2\ \text{MeV}
\]

The explosion yield is \(30\) kilotons TNT equivalent, and

\[
1\ \text{ton TNT} = 4.184\ \text{GJ}
\]

So total explosion energy is

\[
E_{\text{tot}} = 30\,000 \times 4.184\times 10^9\ \text{J}
=1.2552\times 10^{14}\ \text{J}
\]

Convert energy per fission to joules:

\[
E_f = 185.2\times 10^6 \times 1.602\times 10^{-19}
=2.967\times 10^{-11}\ \text{J}
\]

Hence the total number of fissions is

\[
TN=\frac{E_{\text{tot}}}{E_f}
=\frac{1.2552\times 10^{14}}{2.967\times 10^{-11}}
\approx 4.23\times 10^{24}
\]

Now find how much \(^{235}\mathrm U\) actually fissioned:

\[
n(^{235}\mathrm U\ \text{fissioned})=\frac{TN}{N_A}
=\frac{4.23\times 10^{24}}{6.022\times 10^{23}}
\approx 7.03\ \text{mol}
\]

Mass of \(^{235}\mathrm U\) that fissioned:

\[
m_{\text{fissioned}} = 7.03\times 235\ \text{g}
\approx 1650\ \text{g}=1.65\ \text{kg}
\]

Only \(33\%\) of the \(^{235}\mathrm U\) underwent fission, and the uranium was \(90\%\) \(^{235}\mathrm U\) by mass. Therefore

\[
m_{\text{total}}=\frac{1.65}{0.33\times 0.90}
\approx 5.56\ \text{kg}
\]

So,

\[
TN \approx 4.23\times 10^{24}
\]
\[
m \approx 5.56\ \text{kg}
\]

FINAL ANSWER: \(TN \approx 4.23\times 10^{24}\) fissions; \(m \approx 5.56\ \text{kg}\) of 90% enriched uranium.
